s in a "controllable" state. The single 6 is an orphan and will be easily blocked later. The pair of 5s is heavier to handle later.\n\n**My Decision:**\n\nI\'m playing the **Pair of 5s**. This gets rid of two low cards and initiates a pair-focused game, which I have the advantage in due to my pair of Kings, pair of 2s, and the Rocket. My thought process is clear and organized; to eliminate the weaker cards first and consolidate my advantage. This is the optimal start for this round.\n\n', 'output': 'Evaluation: I possess a commanding hand with the Rocket, a pair of 2s, and a pair of Kings. My main weakness is the cluster of low/mid cards (55, 6, J, Q). Since I have the initiative (Free Play), I should eliminate the most restrictive low category. My control in pairs (with KK and 22) is superior to my control in singles (where my Ace can be topped by an opponent\'s 2). Therefore, leading the pair of 5s is the optimal strategy to clear cards and dictate the flow into a favorable channel.\nReason: Leading the lowest pair (55) clears two low cards immediately and forces the opponents to respond with pairs, a track where I hold the dominant 22 and KK. This preserves my flexible high cards (Rocket) for later emergencies or finishing.\nAnswer: {"cards": ["♥5", "♦5"]}'}
